<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- **One entitlement, and pdfium is the whole reason for it.**

     cargo-packager signs the application with `--options runtime`, always and
     without asking: the hardened runtime is what notarisation requires. The
     hardened runtime also turns on *library validation*, which will not load a
     library whose Team ID differs from the process's — and an ad-hoc signature
     has no Team ID at all, so `libpdfium.dylib` in `Contents/Frameworks` is
     refused with "mapping process and mapped file (non-platform) have
     different Team IDs". The reader then opens, shows its start screen, and
     says it cannot open any document.

     Apple's own exception for exactly this case is below. It stays true when
     there is a real Developer ID: pdfium is somebody else's build and will
     never carry our team.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
